//RS /// #1685: the canonical task surface stays resident when its result is a
//RS /// non-`Int` handle, and child failures remain typed at the JIT boundary.
//RS ///
//RS /// D-FAIL-EXIT1=A — fallible entry and one exit law *(ratified 2026-08-06,
//RS /// card #1533)*: "`fn run()` is fallible by default." Entry `fn run` is
//RS /// therefore stamped `Unit !Err`, and a `return` inside a `task { … }` body
//RS /// belongs to the *enclosing* function — `Examples/features/net/http_get.jet`
//RS /// relies on exactly that, where `?? return` inside `task { … }` leaves `run`.
//RS /// So `task { return "child" }` handed a `String` back from `run`: E0113 from
//RS /// the moment D-FAIL-EXIT1 landed, and this fixture (added 2026-08-11,
//RS /// 5e40d7454) was never migrated. `return` is not how a task body yields a
//RS /// value:
//RS ///
//RS /// * A ONE-EXPRESSION brace body folds to `LambdaBody::Expr` — "`task { e }`
//RS ///   and `task e` become the same body"
//RS ///   (`crates/jet-parser/src/Parser/Expressions/primary.rs:768-795`). Any
//RS ///   expression is fine there, so `task { "child" }` is accepted.
//RS /// * A MULTI-STATEMENT brace body takes the `block_stmts` path
//RS ///   (primary.rs:796-802) and has NO tail-value form: the parser never sets
//RS ///   `callable_tail_block_depth` for it, so the tail carve-out at
//RS ///   `Parser/Statements/control.rs:2547-2550` cannot fire, and a bare
//RS ///   expression statement is admitted only when it is `Expr::Call`, `Field`,
//RS ///   `MethodCall`, `ComptimeName`, `Try`, `OrFallback`, or `IncDec`
//RS ///   (control.rs:2528-2537). `concurrency/freeze_capture.jet:13` and
//RS ///   `scoped_borrow_bands.jet:35` only look like tail values because
//RS ///   `Expr::Field` is in that set. A bare literal is not, and a `Str` token
//RS ///   never even reaches that match (control.rs:2585) — hence E0003. No shipped
//RS ///   example gives a task a literal result, so the form is unexercised.
//RS ///
//RS /// So a delayed String result uses the corpus shape: a named helper called from
//RS /// the folded one-expression body, exactly as `concurrency/race_cancel.jet:13`
//RS /// and `all_failfast.jet:30-33` spell their slow children.
//RS ///
//RS /// The `return` also drove the E0107 cascade over every later binding in the
//RS /// `task.group` block: `check_stmt` marks the block unreachable after a
//RS /// `Stmt::Return` and then rolls `self.flow` back for each following statement
//RS /// (`crates/jet-sema/src/Sema/CheckerCore/statements.rs:366-372`), discarding
//RS /// their declarations. Nothing restores that flag around a lambda body
//RS /// (`CheckerInfer/calls/lambdas.rs:609`), so the task body's `return` leaked out.
//RS ///
//RS /// The two claims below were unverified until the fixture above was migrated,
//RS /// and both were wrong on first run.
//RS ///
//RS /// 1. `print(all_result[0], all_result[1])` writes TWO lines, not one.
//RS ///    D-VERDICT-1321-1 *(ratified 2026-07-30, amends S9 print arity;
//RS ///    `Docs/spec/syntax-decisions.md:6147-6150`)*: `print` "accept[s] one or
//RS ///    more arguments and write[s] each argument on its own line, in order, with
//RS ///    a trailing newline after the last." The shipped corpus prints a
//RS ///    `task.all` result in exactly this spelling — `task_all.jet:16` is
//RS ///    `print(results[0], results[1], results[2])` and its golden
//RS ///    `expected/concurrency/task_all.out` is three lines. So the fixture is
//RS ///    corpus-shaped and the old one-line `"left right"` expectation, written
//RS ///    2026-08-11 (twelve days after the decision) and never once executed, was
//RS ///    the defect. The fixture is NOT reshaped to interpolate a joined line:
//RS ///    that would stop covering variadic `print` over a task-combinator result.
//RS ///
//RS /// 2. `panic("boom")`, not `panic("child")`. Both strings were `"child"`, so
//RS ///    the child's re-raised panic message was indistinguishable from the
//RS ///    `task { "child" }` result value in any runtime evidence. They are
//RS ///    separate slots — `RICH_PANIC_REASON` (`jet-jit/src/Concurrency.rs`) has
//RS ///    one writer, `jet_jit_rich_panic` (`jit/runtime_host.rs`), which stores a
//RS ///    panic message and never a task result — but the fixture should not make
//RS ///    that take a proof to see.
//RS #[test]
//RS fn task_surface_runs_resident_with_string_results_and_typed_failures() {
//RS     if skip_if_cranelift_host_unsupported() {
//RS         return;
//RS     }
//RS     let source = r#"
